WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.190
Our class drew our town's

2
00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.630
rainfall as a pie chart.

3
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.600
Twelve slices, one for each month.

4
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.510
Nobody could see when the rains came.

5
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.980
The data was right, but

6
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.220
the chart was wrong.

7
00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.810
Today: which chart fits which

8
00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.530
question, and how charts can mislead.

9
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.560
A bar chart compares groups.

10
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.810
Which fruit does our class like best?

11
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.060
One bar for each fruit, and

12
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.220
the tallest bar wins.

13
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.770
Mango wins easily here,

14
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.170
with eleven pupils.

15
00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.090
A line chart shows change over time.

16
00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:42.350
Rainfall month by month makes a line that

17
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.030
rises in the rainy season and falls again.

18
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.860
Now everyone can see when the rains come.

19
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.620
A pie chart shows how one whole is

20
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.720
shared out, like the school budget:

21
00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.080
books, repairs, sports and food.

22
00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.950
Use it only with a few slices

23
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.670
that add up to the whole.

24
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.560
A scatter chart puts two measurements of

25
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.900
each pupil on one picture, like hours of

26
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.480
reading and spelling marks.

27
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.830
Each dot is one pupil.

28
00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.970
If the dots rise together,

29
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.850
the two go together.

30
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.310
Now a trick to watch for.

31
00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.450
Our pass rate went from seventy

32
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.650
to seventy four percent.

33
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.050
If the axis starts at sixty eight, the

34
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.630
new bar looks three times taller.

35
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.970
Bars must start at zero.

36
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:37.810
Quick check!

37
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.580
You measured a bean plant

38
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:43.060
every day for two weeks.

39
00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.170
Which chart shows how it grew?

40
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.200
A line chart.

41
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.130
The days go along the bottom, the

42
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.610
height goes up, and the line shows

43
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.930
the growth over time.

44
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.850
You can even see the days

45
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:57.750
when it grew fastest.

46
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.980
In the app, open Pick the right chart.

47
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.810
Match five questions to their charts,

48
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.010
then drag where the axis starts and

49
00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.290
watch a small change look huge.

50
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.160
Your turn.

51
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.410
A poster shows two bars, and

52
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.970
the axis starts at ninety.

53
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.050
What should you check first?

54
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.700
Where the axis starts.

55
00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.110
Remember: bars compare groups, lines show

56
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.430
time, pies show parts of a whole, and

57
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.110
scatter shows two measurements.

58
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.860
And bars start at zero.

59
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.130
Try it yourself: draw a bar chart of your

60
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.690
family's favourite foods, with a title,

61
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.930
labels and a scale from zero.

62
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.720
Show it to someone and ask what

63
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:46.620
they can tell from it.
